Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### psql postgres://postgres:root@localhost:5432/blog_db
### SELECT * FROM sessions;


### Access a Protected Route With Active Session & Enabled User
### Expected: 201 Created (or 200 OK depending on your POST /api/blogs handler).
### Proves: Valid JWT + active `sessions` row + `users.disabled = false` succeeds.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "title": "Session Check Before Logout",
  "author": "Test User",
  "url": "https://example.com/session-before-logout",
  "likes": 1
}


### Logout -> Deletes Active Session Row From `sessions` Table
### Expected: 204 No Content.
### Verify in psql: SELECT * FROM sessions; (session row for @token is gone)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### Reuse the Same Token After Logout (Must Fail Even Though JWT Is Valid)
### Expected: 401 Unauthorized with { "error": "session invalid" }.
### Proves: Server-side session check blocks logged-out tokens immediately.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "title": "Should Fail After Logout",
  "author": "Test User",
  "url": "https://example.com/session-after-logout",
  "likes": 1
}


### Log In Again & Disable the User in PostgreSQL
### Action:
###   a) Run POST /api/login below and paste the fresh `token` into `@token`.
###   b) In psql, run:
###        UPDATE users SET disabled = true WHERE username = 'testuser@example.com';
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### Attempt Protected Route While User Is Disabled (With Existing Token)
### Expected: 403 Forbidden with { "error": "account disabled" }.
### Proves: Even an active session / valid JWT is rejected once disabled = true.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "title": "Should Fail When Disabled",
  "author": "Test User",
  "url": "https://example.com/disabled-user-request",
  "likes": 1
}


### Attempt Login While User Is Disabled
### Expected: 401 Unauthorized with { "error": "account disabled" }.
### Proves: Disabled accounts cannot start new sessions or receive new tokens.
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### Re-Enable User in PostgreSQL & Confirm Login Works Again
### Action:
###   a) In psql, run:
###        UPDATE users SET disabled = false WHERE username = 'testuser@example.com';
###   b) Run POST /api/login below.
### Expected: 200 OK with a new `token`, and a new row appears in `sessions`.
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

